This is most likely about lab-process-email but not necessarily. It's a
broader idea - but I think it's working towards an implementation change
here.

There are three primary sources of context for my email patch workflow:

- trunk@upstream
- b/dev@upstream
- mail@upstream

The first two are pretty obvious: they are the latest common immutable
branch, and my personal dev branch that is a staging area for trunk
candidates.

The second is a bit more interesting. I don't know what to call it,
because it's not necessarily work in progress... it's just another
source of commits, plus the communication messages.

For the commits specifically, we can think of them as being differing
degrees of formal, mature, or durable:

- trunk@upstream - commits published for the world to see
- b/dev@upstream - my personal tree, published for interested parties,
  but not guaranteed to be maintained as is
- mail@upstream - commits that have been shared. Some applied, some
  rejected, some not yet really considered

The goal for any dev work is to flow from mail@upstream to
trunk@upstream. Not everything will make it of course - but one of the
success criteria of how well the system performs is the % rate at which
commits are accepted. All commits should eventually have a resolution -
but in an ideal world, the first patch series is accepted and committed.

I bring this up because I want this idea represented in the code or
prompt or config. Not necessarily the formality - but that there are
three sources. The tool should prompt agent with that info, so it knows
that it can e.g. consult with mail@upstream for other work, but not
necessarily rely on it.

Also, there's thinking about input / output channels. Essentially right
now mail@upstream is a duplex io channel. That's fine, and I don't
necessarily have any plans to change it or add other channels over
time. But I want that abstraction to be represented in the system, and
not have a hard coupling to email.
